14 letters outside Northern Ireland's  Parliament belly seem enough to conveyed  this historic moment for years  campaigners have fought for same-sex  marriage to be legalized and for  abortion in all cases to be allowed and  from midnight they will have won it  marks the end of inequality and  discrimination both for same-sex couples  who have longed wanted to marry the  person they love and also for a women  who have been suffering at the hands of  our abortion ban this change  particularly to the abortion law is  controversial it was brought in by  Westminster MPs and is very mentally  opposed by the Democratic Unionist Party  here but with the power showing  government still not functioning in  Northern Ireland there was no way they  could stop it  it is a very sad day and I know some  people will seek to celebrate it today  and I would say to those people think of  those of us who are sad today and who  believe that this is an affront to human  dignity and to human life but  campaigners did celebrate with wedding  cake and photo opportunities for those  who can now legally marry as for the  abortion laws it still needs to be  worked out exactly what services will be  provided in Northern Ireland and how  they will be provided to but what this  law changes almost immediately is that  women who go abroad for abortions or who  buy abortion pills online as well as the  doctors that give them advice about  seeking an abortion can no longer be  prosecuted and it will mean eventually  an end to stories like Sarah's who had  to go to England for a termination could  be sent away somewhere where we didn't  know and nobody knew in medical history  was a very scary thing unfortunately I  have nuclear to visit my little girl  knew ashes in my home that would be  something that people from Nile will  have the option of having if because we  were able to get the treatment here at  home  last year voters in the Republic of  Ireland chose to end their abortion ban  different polls showed different results  in Northern Ireland it's still a  divisive issue that people often choose  abortions for personal social  convenience reasons and really don't  want to see that happen here I have two  daughters now at one seven one three and  knowing that they will never be in  circumstances where they need an  abortion and can't access this here at  home is a huge relief and the debate  doesn't end here with negotiations to  come over where and how abortion  services will be provided Caterina  vertices Sky News in Belfast
 

